/* PrinceXmahto Study — complete Semester-I topic layer
   Syllabus-aligned and topic-specific. No repeated generic chapter filler.
*/
(function(){
 const L=window.PX_LESSONS||(window.PX_LESSONS={});
 const N=(overview,sections,questions=[],revision='')=>({overview,sections,questions,revision});
 const add=(name,overview,sections,q,rev)=>L[name]=N(overview,sections,q,rev);

add('Water','Water is an essential engineering resource. This topic covers hardness, its types, effects, determination and common treatment methods used to obtain water suitable for domestic and industrial purposes.',[
['1. Hardness of Water','<p><b>Hardness</b> is the property of water that prevents easy formation of lather with soap. It is mainly caused by dissolved calcium and magnesium salts.</p><p>Hardness is classified as <b>temporary</b> and <b>permanent</b>. Temporary hardness is mainly associated with bicarbonates of calcium and magnesium and can be reduced by boiling. Permanent hardness is associated with sulphates, chlorides and related salts and is not removed simply by boiling.</p>'],
['2. Effects of Hard Water','<p>Hard water wastes soap by forming insoluble scum. In boilers it may produce scale and sludge, reducing heat transfer and increasing fuel consumption. Deposits can also restrict water flow and contribute to maintenance problems.</p><p>Hardness is therefore an important consideration in boiler feed water, cooling systems and industrial processing.</p>'],
['3. EDTA Method','<p>The EDTA complexometric method determines hardness by forming stable complexes with Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions. In the common procedure, the sample is buffered to the required alkaline condition and an indicator such as Eriochrome Black T is used. EDTA is added until the characteristic end-point colour change is observed.</p><div class="formula">Hardness as CaCO₃ (mg/L) = (V × M × 100000) / Vs</div><p>where V is the volume of standard EDTA used, M is its molarity/equivalent concentration according to the laboratory procedure, and Vs is sample volume. The exact calculation convention must match the standardisation method used in the laboratory.</p>'],
['4. Softening of Water','<p>Common softening methods include lime-soda treatment, zeolite/permutit process and ion-exchange or demineralisation methods. The choice depends on required water quality and application.</p><p><b>Ion exchange:</b> cation and anion exchange resins replace unwanted ions and can produce highly purified water. In industrial plants, treatment may be combined with filtration, disinfection and other processes.</p>']],['★ Define temporary and permanent hardness.','★ Explain the EDTA method and role of EBT indicator.','★ State the effects of hard water in boilers.','★ Compare zeolite and ion-exchange methods.'],'Hardness → types → effects → determination → softening.' );

add('Engineering Materials','Engineering materials are selected according to strength, hardness, toughness, ductility, corrosion resistance, temperature resistance, weight, cost and manufacturability.',[
['1. Classification','<p>Engineering materials are broadly classified as <b>metals and alloys, polymers, ceramics, composites</b> and advanced functional materials. Metals may be ferrous or non-ferrous. Ferrous materials contain iron as the principal constituent; steels and cast irons are major examples.</p>'],
['2. Important Mechanical Properties','<p><b>Strength</b> is resistance to failure under load. <b>Hardness</b> is resistance to indentation, scratching or wear. <b>Toughness</b> is the ability to absorb energy before fracture. <b>Ductility</b> is the ability to undergo plastic deformation in tension. <b>Brittleness</b> describes materials that fracture with little plastic deformation.</p><p>Elasticity describes recovery after removal of load, while plasticity describes permanent deformation.</p>'],
['3. Ferrous and Non-Ferrous Materials','<p>Steel is an iron-carbon alloy whose properties are modified by carbon content and alloying elements. Cast iron contains more carbon and is valued for castability, compressive strength and damping in suitable applications.</p><p>Aluminium is light and corrosion resistant; copper has excellent electrical and thermal conductivity; brass and bronze are important copper-based alloys.</p>'],
['4. Material Selection','<p>Material selection is a design decision. A component exposed to cyclic load may require fatigue resistance; a cutting tool needs high hardness and wear resistance; a conductor requires suitable electrical conductivity. Cost, availability, manufacturing process and environmental conditions must also be considered.</p>']],['★ Classify engineering materials.','★ Differentiate strength, hardness, toughness and ductility.','★ Compare ferrous and non-ferrous materials.','★ Explain factors affecting material selection.'],'Material → properties → service conditions → manufacturing → cost and sustainability.' );

add('Chemistry of Fuel and Lubricants','Fuels release useful energy through controlled chemical reactions, while lubricants reduce friction, wear and heat between moving surfaces.',[
['1. Fuel and Calorific Value','<p>A <b>fuel</b> is a substance that releases useful heat energy on combustion. The <b>calorific value</b> is the heat released by complete combustion of a unit quantity of fuel under specified conditions.</p><p>Higher calorific value includes the latent heat associated with condensation of water vapour in combustion products; lower calorific value excludes that recovered condensation heat.</p>'],
['2. Solid, Liquid and Gaseous Fuels','<p>Coal and coke are solid fuels. Petrol, diesel and kerosene are common liquid fuels. LPG, CNG and other gases are gaseous fuels. Selection depends on energy density, storage, handling, combustion control, emissions and cost.</p>'],
['3. Lubrication','<p>Lubrication creates a separating film between moving surfaces and reduces direct metal-to-metal contact. It reduces friction, wear and temperature and may also protect surfaces from corrosion.</p><p>Lubricants may be classified as liquid oils, greases and solid lubricants. Important properties include viscosity, viscosity index, flash point, fire point, pour point and chemical stability.</p>'],
['4. Viscosity and Lubricant Selection','<p><b>Viscosity</b> represents resistance to flow. A lubricant must maintain an adequate film at operating temperature without producing unnecessary viscous losses. High-speed and heavily loaded mechanisms can require different lubricant characteristics.</p>']],['★ Define calorific value.','★ Compare solid, liquid and gaseous fuels.','★ Explain the purpose of lubrication.','★ Define viscosity, flash point and fire point.'],'Fuel supplies energy; lubricant controls friction, wear and heat.' );

add('Electrochemistry','Electrochemistry deals with chemical reactions involving transfer of electrons and the conversion between chemical and electrical energy.',[
['1. Oxidation and Reduction','<p><b>Oxidation</b> involves loss of electrons, while <b>reduction</b> involves gain of electrons. They always occur together in a redox reaction.</p><div class="formula">Oxidation: M → Mⁿ⁺ + ne⁻<br>Reduction: Xⁿ⁺ + ne⁻ → X</div>'],
['2. Electrolytes and Electrodes','<p>An <b>electrolyte</b> conducts electricity through mobile ions. Electrodes provide surfaces at which oxidation or reduction occurs. The electrode where oxidation occurs is the <b>anode</b>; the electrode where reduction occurs is the <b>cathode</b>.</p>'],
['3. Galvanic and Electrolytic Cells','<p>A galvanic cell converts spontaneous chemical energy into electrical energy. An electrolytic cell uses external electrical energy to drive a non-spontaneous chemical reaction.</p><p>The sign convention for electrodes differs between galvanic and electrolytic cells, but oxidation is always at the anode and reduction is always at the cathode.</p>'],
['4. Nernst Equation and Applications','<p>The electrode potential depends on temperature, concentration and reaction quotient. The Nernst equation provides this dependence.</p><div class="formula">E = E° − (RT/nF) ln Q</div><p>At 25°C it is commonly written using log₁₀ in the equivalent form. Electrochemical principles are used in batteries, electroplating, corrosion control and sensors.</p>']],['★ Define oxidation and reduction.','★ Identify anode and cathode.','★ Differentiate galvanic and electrolytic cells.','★ State the Nernst equation and explain its terms.'],'Redox → electrode reactions → cell potential → electrochemical applications.' );

add('Introduction to Thermodynamics','Thermodynamics studies energy, heat and work and the transformations between them in physical systems.',[
['1. System, Surroundings and Boundary','<p>A <b>system</b> is the part selected for study; everything outside is the surroundings. The boundary separates them. A closed system permits energy transfer but not mass transfer across its boundary, while an open system can exchange both mass and energy.</p>'],
['2. Properties and State','<p>Pressure, temperature, volume and specific volume are examples of thermodynamic properties. A state is specified when enough independent properties are known. Intensive properties do not depend on system size; extensive properties depend on the amount of matter.</p>'],
['3. Heat and Work','<p>Heat is energy transfer caused by temperature difference. Work is energy transfer associated with a force or generalized displacement. Neither heat nor work is stored as a property of a system; they describe energy crossing the boundary.</p>'],
['4. First Law','<p>The first law expresses conservation of energy. For a simple closed system under a common sign convention:</p><div class="formula">δQ = dU + δW</div><p>For a process, heat supplied equals increase in internal energy plus work done by the system. Sign conventions must be stated before solving numerical problems.</p>'],
['5. Second Law and Entropy','<p>The second law gives the direction of natural processes and introduces entropy. No heat engine can convert all supplied heat into work in a cyclic process. Entropy is a state property and is useful for analysing irreversibility and efficiency.</p>']],['★ Define system, surroundings and boundary.','★ Distinguish intensive and extensive properties.','★ State the first law of thermodynamics.','★ Explain the significance of entropy.'],'System → state → energy transfer → first law → direction and irreversibility.' );

add('Internal Combustion Engine and Refrigeration','IC engines convert chemical energy of fuel into mechanical work inside the engine cylinder, while refrigeration systems remove heat from a low-temperature region and reject it to a higher-temperature region.',[
['1. IC Engine Principle','<p>In an internal combustion engine, fuel burns inside the working cylinder. The high-pressure gases produced by combustion act on the piston and produce mechanical work through the connecting rod and crank mechanism.</p>'],
['2. Four-Stroke Engine','<ol><li><b>Intake:</b> fresh charge enters the cylinder.</li><li><b>Compression:</b> charge is compressed.</li><li><b>Power:</b> combustion produces high-pressure gases and the piston moves.</li><li><b>Exhaust:</b> burnt gases leave the cylinder.</li></ol><p>A four-stroke cycle requires two crankshaft revolutions for one power stroke.</p>'],
['3. SI and CI Engines','<p>In an SI engine, the air-fuel mixture is ignited by a spark. In a CI engine, air is compressed to a high temperature and fuel is injected into the hot compressed air, initiating combustion.</p><p>Engine selection depends on speed, load, fuel, efficiency, emissions and application.</p>'],
['4. Refrigeration','<p>Refrigeration is the process of maintaining a space at a temperature below its surroundings by continuously removing heat. In a vapour-compression system, the main components are compressor, condenser, expansion device and evaporator.</p><div class="formula">COP of refrigerator = Refrigerating effect / Work input</div><p>The evaporator absorbs heat at low temperature, the compressor raises pressure, the condenser rejects heat and the expansion device reduces pressure.</p>']],['★ Explain four strokes of a four-stroke engine.','★ Differentiate SI and CI engines.','★ Draw and explain the vapour-compression refrigeration cycle.','★ Define coefficient of performance.'],'Engine: intake → compression → power → exhaust. Refrigerator: evaporator → compressor → condenser → expansion device.' );

add('Manufacturing Processes and Machine Tools','Manufacturing converts raw material into useful components by processes such as casting, forming, joining and machining.',[
['1. Casting','<p>In casting, molten material is poured into a mould cavity and allowed to solidify. A pattern is used to create the cavity. Important elements include mould, core where required, gating system and riser.</p><p>Common defects include blowholes, shrinkage cavities, misruns and cold shuts. Good mould design, proper pouring temperature and controlled solidification reduce defects.</p>'],
['2. Metal Forming','<p>Forging shapes metal by compressive forces. Rolling reduces thickness or changes cross-section between rotating rolls. Extrusion forces material through a die. Drawing pulls material through a die to reduce cross-section.</p><p>Hot working occurs above a suitable recrystallisation temperature and permits large deformation with reduced work hardening. Cold working improves strength and surface finish but may increase residual stress.</p>'],
['3. Welding and Joining','<p>Welding creates a permanent joint by coalescence of materials with heat, pressure or both. Arc welding uses an electric arc as a heat source. Gas welding uses combustion of a fuel gas with oxygen. Soldering and brazing use filler materials at lower temperatures than fusion welding.</p>'],
['4. Lathe Machine','<p>A lathe rotates the workpiece while a cutting tool removes material. Basic operations include turning, facing, taper turning, threading, drilling and knurling.</p><p>Cutting speed, feed and depth of cut strongly affect productivity, tool life and surface finish.</p>'],
['5. Other Machine Tools','<p>Milling uses a rotating multi-point cutter. Drilling produces holes using a rotating drill. Shaping and planing produce surfaces using reciprocating cutting motion. Grinding uses an abrasive wheel for high dimensional accuracy and surface finish.</p>']],['★ Explain the principle of casting.','★ Compare hot and cold working.','★ Explain arc welding and gas welding.','★ Draw and label a lathe and list its operations.','Differentiate lathe, milling, drilling, shaping and grinding.'],'Material removal/addition/forming → machine selection → cutting conditions → inspection.' );

add('Power Transmission','Power transmission transfers mechanical power from a prime mover to a driven machine while providing the required speed, torque and direction.',[
['1. Belt Drive','<p>A belt drive transmits power by friction between a belt and pulleys. It is suitable for relatively large centre distances and provides smooth operation. Slip and creep can affect the speed ratio.</p><div class="formula">For an ideal belt drive: N₁D₁ ≈ N₂D₂</div>'],
['2. Gear Drive','<p>Gears transmit motion through meshing teeth and provide a positive drive with no ordinary slip. Spur gears are used for parallel shafts; bevel gears transmit between intersecting shafts; worm gears can provide large speed reduction.</p><div class="formula">Gear ratio = driven speed relation to driver size/teeth according to selected convention</div>'],
['3. Chain Drive','<p>Chain drives transmit power through engagement between chain links and sprocket teeth. They are useful where positive drive is required and moderate centre distances are involved.</p>'],
['4. Selection and Efficiency','<p>Drive selection depends on power, speed, centre distance, required speed ratio, noise, maintenance and environment. Mechanical efficiency is the ratio of useful output power to input power.</p><div class="formula">η = Pout/Pin × 100%</div>']],['★ Explain belt drive and slip.','★ Compare belt, chain and gear drives.','★ State the speed relation for a simple belt drive.','★ Define mechanical efficiency.'],'Belt = flexible friction drive; chain = positive flexible drive; gear = positive toothed drive.' );

add('Communication','Communication is the systematic exchange of information, ideas and feelings between a sender and receiver through a suitable channel.',[
['1. Communication Process','<p>The basic process contains <b>sender → encoding → message → channel → receiver → decoding → feedback</b>. Noise can interfere at any stage.</p><p>Encoding converts an idea into symbols or words. Decoding gives meaning to the received message. Feedback shows whether the message has been understood.</p>'],
['2. Verbal and Non-Verbal Communication','<p>Verbal communication uses spoken or written language. Non-verbal communication includes facial expression, posture, gestures, eye contact and other behavioural signals. Effective professional communication requires consistency between verbal and non-verbal signals.</p>'],
['3. Barriers','<p>Common barriers include language difficulty, poor listening, physical noise, emotional state, cultural differences, information overload and ambiguous wording. Barriers are reduced by clear language, active listening, appropriate channel selection and confirmation of understanding.</p>'],
['4. 7 Cs of Communication','<p>The commonly taught 7 Cs are <b>clear, concise, concrete, correct, coherent, complete and courteous</b>. They provide a practical checklist for professional messages.</p>']],['★ Draw the communication process.','★ Explain verbal and non-verbal communication.','★ List communication barriers.','★ Explain the 7 Cs.'],'Sender → message → channel → receiver → feedback; reduce noise and ambiguity.' );

add('Types of Communication','Communication can be classified by medium, direction, formality and number of participants.',[
['1. Oral Communication','<p>Oral communication includes face-to-face discussion, telephone calls, interviews, meetings and presentations. It provides quick feedback and allows tone and emphasis to support meaning.</p>'],
['2. Written Communication','<p>Written communication includes letters, notices, reports, emails, applications and technical documents. It creates a durable record and requires careful grammar, structure and formatting.</p>'],
['3. Formal and Informal Communication','<p>Formal communication follows organisational roles and established channels. Informal communication develops through personal interaction and may be faster, but professional information should still be verified before action.</p>'],
['4. Upward, Downward and Horizontal','<p><b>Downward</b> communication moves from higher authority to lower levels. <b>Upward</b> communication carries information, feedback or reports toward higher authority. <b>Horizontal</b> communication occurs between people or units at similar levels.</p>']],['★ Compare oral and written communication.','★ Explain formal and informal communication.','★ Define upward, downward and horizontal communication.'],'Choose the medium according to urgency, complexity, record requirement and audience.' );

add('Reading Comprehension','Reading comprehension means understanding, analysing and interpreting written information rather than merely reading individual words.',[
['1. Reading for Meaning','<p>Begin by identifying the topic, purpose and main idea. Then locate supporting details and determine how each paragraph contributes to the overall message.</p>'],
['2. Skimming and Scanning','<p><b>Skimming</b> is rapid reading to understand the overall idea. <b>Scanning</b> is searching for a specific fact, name, number or keyword. Both are useful study skills but have different purposes.</p>'],
['3. Inference and Context','<p>An inference is a conclusion supported by information in the passage even when it is not stated word-for-word. Context helps determine the meaning of unfamiliar words. Avoid choosing an answer that is merely possible; it should be supported by the passage.</p>'],
['4. Exam Method','<ol><li>Read the title and questions.</li><li>Read the passage carefully.</li><li>Underline key facts mentally or on paper.</li><li>Answer from the passage, not from unrelated assumptions.</li><li>Check that the answer matches the exact question.</li></ol>']],['★ Define skimming and scanning.','★ What is inference?','★ Explain the steps used to solve a comprehension passage.'],'Main idea + supporting detail + context + inference = effective comprehension.' );

add('Vocabulary and Grammar','Professional English requires accurate word choice, sentence structure and grammatical control.',[
['1. Parts of Speech','<p>The major parts of speech include noun, pronoun, verb, adjective, adverb, preposition, conjunction and interjection. Their function in a sentence determines how words should be used.</p>'],
['2. Tense and Agreement','<p>Verb tense indicates time and aspect. Subject-verb agreement requires the verb to agree with the grammatical subject in number and person.</p><p>Example: “The machine <b>works</b> efficiently.” “The machines <b>work</b> efficiently.”</p>'],
['3. Articles and Prepositions','<p>Articles <b>a, an, the</b> help identify nouns. Prepositions express relationships such as place, time, direction and association. Correct usage depends on context and common grammatical patterns.</p>'],
['4. Technical Vocabulary','<p>Technical writing prefers precise words. Avoid unnecessary slang, vague expressions and ambiguous pronouns. Define specialised terms when the intended reader may not know them.</p>']],['★ Explain parts of speech.','★ Explain subject-verb agreement with examples.','★ Use articles and prepositions correctly.','★ Why is precise vocabulary important in technical writing?'],'Grammar controls structure; vocabulary controls precision.' );

add('Professional Writing','Professional writing communicates information accurately to a defined audience and purpose.',[
['1. Email Writing','<p>A professional email normally contains a meaningful subject line, greeting, concise purpose, relevant details, clear action/request and appropriate closing. Avoid unnecessary abbreviations and excessive informal language.</p>'],
['2. Notice and Application','<p>A notice should present the event, date, time, venue and instructions clearly. A formal application identifies the recipient, subject, purpose, relevant details and respectful closing.</p>'],
['3. Report Writing','<p>A basic report can contain title, introduction/objective, method or background, observations/findings, discussion, conclusion and recommendations where applicable. Headings and concise paragraphs improve readability.</p>'],
['4. Technical Writing Principles','<p>Use measurable statements where possible, define abbreviations, maintain consistent terminology and distinguish facts from assumptions. Tables, diagrams and bullet lists should support rather than replace necessary explanation.</p>']],['★ Write the format of a professional email.','★ Explain the structure of a report.','★ What makes technical writing effective?','★ Draft a formal application.'],'Purpose → audience → structure → precise language → review.' );

add('Basic Elements of Drawing','Engineering drawing is a standard graphical language used to communicate the shape, size and specifications of engineering objects.',[
['1. Drawing Instruments and Lines','<p>Common instruments include drawing board, T-square, set squares, compass, divider, scale and pencils of different grades. Line conventions distinguish visible outlines, hidden details, centre lines, dimension lines and construction lines.</p>'],
['2. Lettering and Dimensioning','<p>Engineering lettering should be uniform and legible. Dimensioning communicates size using dimension lines, extension lines, arrowheads and numerical values. Dimensions should be placed clearly without ambiguity or unnecessary duplication.</p>'],
['3. Scales','<p>A scale represents an object at a chosen ratio. <b>Full-size</b> scale has RF 1:1; reduced scale represents the object smaller; enlarged scale represents it larger.</p><div class="formula">RF = length on drawing / actual length</div>'],
['4. Geometrical Construction','<p>Basic constructions include perpendiculars, parallel lines, angle bisection, regular polygons and tangent constructions. Accurate construction lines are important because later views depend on them.</p>']],['★ State common drawing instruments.','★ Explain line conventions.','★ Define RF and types of scales.','★ Explain basic dimensioning rules.'],'Drawing language = lines + lettering + scale + dimensions + standard conventions.' );

add('Orthographic Projections','Orthographic projection represents a three-dimensional object through two-dimensional views obtained by projecting points perpendicular to reference planes.',[
['1. Principle','<p>The principal views are commonly <b>front view/elevation, top view/plan and side view/end view</b>. Each view shows two of the three principal dimensions and together the views describe the object.</p>'],
['2. First-Angle Method','<p>In first-angle projection, the object is positioned between the observer and the plane of projection. The relative placement of views follows the standard first-angle convention used by the syllabus when specified.</p>'],
['3. Converting Pictorial to Orthographic','<p>First identify the front face and overall dimensions. Project construction lines vertically and horizontally to locate corresponding features in other views. Transfer widths, heights and depths carefully. Finally darken visible outlines and apply hidden/centre lines where required.</p>'],
['4. Common Features','<p>Slots, holes, cylindrical surfaces, steps, ribs and inclined surfaces must appear consistently in the related views. A circular feature may appear as a circle in one view and as a rectangle or line depending on its orientation.</p>']],['★ Define orthographic projection.','★ Explain first-angle projection.','★ Convert a simple pictorial object into three orthographic views.','★ Explain the relationship between front, top and side views.'],'Front + top + side views together describe the 3D object accurately.' );

add('Isometric Projection','Isometric drawing is a pictorial method for showing three dimensions in one view. The three principal isometric axes are equally inclined in the standard representation.',[
['1. Isometric Axes','<p>One axis is vertical and the other two are drawn at approximately 30° to the horizontal. Lines parallel to these axes are called isometric lines.</p>'],
['2. Isometric Scale','<p>In true isometric projection, lengths are foreshortened. In practical isometric drawing, an isometric scale may be used. Do not confuse an isometric view made with true lengths with a geometrically projected isometric representation.</p>'],
['3. Construction Method','<ol><li>Draw the three isometric axes.</li><li>Construct the overall bounding box.</li><li>Transfer width, depth and height.</li><li>Locate holes, slots and other features.</li><li>Draw circles as isometric ellipses.</li><li>Darken visible edges and remove unnecessary construction lines.</li></ol>'],
['4. Orthographic to Isometric','<p>Read the front, top and side views together. Establish the overall width, depth and height before adding details. Every feature must be located using its position in the orthographic views.</p>']],['★ Define isometric axes.','★ Differentiate isometric projection and isometric drawing.','★ Explain the step-by-step construction of an isometric view.','★ Convert given orthographic views into an isometric representation.'],'Three axes → bounding box → dimensions → features → visible edges.' );

add('Free Hand Sketches of Engineering Elements','Free-hand engineering sketching develops the ability to communicate shape without depending on full drawing instruments.',[
['1. Sketching Principles','<p>Begin with light construction lines and establish proportions before darkening the final outline. Use repeated strokes rather than forcing one perfect line.</p>'],
['2. Common Sections','<p>Practise square, rectangular, circular, hollow, L, T, U and I-shaped sections. Keep parallel faces, symmetry and relative proportions consistent.</p>'],
['3. Machine and Engineering Elements','<p>Simple bolts, nuts, shafts, brackets, flanges, keys and common machine features can be represented using standard conventions. The aim is clarity, proportion and recognition rather than artistic shading.</p>']],['★ Explain the purpose of free-hand engineering sketching.','★ Sketch common engineering sections.','★ List the principles of a good engineering sketch.'],'Light construction → proportion → details → final visible outline.' );

add('Basic Computer Aided Drafting','Computer-aided drafting uses CAD software to create accurate digital drawings using coordinate systems, layers, drawing commands and editing tools.',[
['1. CAD Workspace','<p>A CAD system normally provides drawing area, command interface, coordinate input, layers, object properties and viewing controls. Units and drawing limits should be set before detailed work.</p>'],
['2. Basic Commands','<p>Common 2D commands include <b>line, circle, arc, rectangle, polygon, trim, extend, offset, mirror, move, copy, rotate</b> and dimension tools. Exact command names can vary between CAD packages.</p>'],
['3. Layers and Dimensions','<p>Layers organise objects such as construction geometry, object outlines, centre lines and dimensions. Consistent layer properties make drawings easier to edit and print.</p>'],
['4. CAD Workflow','<p>Set units → create layers → draw construction geometry → add object geometry → edit/trim → dimension → inspect → save/export. Accuracy depends on coordinates, object snaps and disciplined layer use.</p>']],['★ State advantages of CAD.','★ Explain layers.','★ List basic drawing and modify commands.','★ Explain a simple 2D CAD workflow.'],'Set up → construct → edit → dimension → inspect → output.' );

add('Advanced Computer Aided Drafting','Advanced drafting extends basic CAD work through precise editing, blocks, annotation, layouts and more organised drawing production.',[
['1. Precision Tools','<p>Object snaps help select exact geometric points such as endpoint, midpoint, centre and intersection. Ortho and polar tracking constrain cursor movement to useful directions.</p>'],
['2. Blocks and Reusable Content','<p>Blocks group related geometry into reusable objects. They are useful for repeated symbols such as electrical or architectural elements and reduce repetitive editing.</p>'],
['3. Annotation and Layout','<p>Dimensions, text, leaders and symbols communicate design intent. Layouts and viewports control how model geometry is arranged for plotting at a selected scale.</p>'],
['4. Drawing Quality','<p>A professional CAD drawing requires consistent layers, lineweights, text height, dimensions, units and naming. Before plotting, inspect geometry for gaps, duplicate lines, incorrect scales and missing annotations.</p>']],['★ Explain object snaps.','★ What is a CAD block?','★ Explain the purpose of layout and viewport.','★ List checks before plotting a drawing.'],'Accuracy + organisation + annotation + plotting control.' );

add('Workshop Safety and Measuring Tools','Workshop safety protects people, tools and equipment and creates reliable working habits.',[
['1. General Safety','<p>Wear appropriate PPE, keep the work area clean, use the correct tool for the job and never operate unfamiliar machinery without instruction. Guards and safety devices should remain in place.</p>'],
['2. Measuring Tools','<p>Steel rule provides simple linear measurement. Vernier caliper measures external, internal and depth dimensions with greater precision. Micrometer provides finer measurement for suitable ranges. Always check zero error and avoid excessive measuring force.</p>'],
['3. Good Workshop Practice','<p>Secure the workpiece, inspect tools before use, keep cutting edges controlled and remove swarf using safe methods. Do not use damaged electrical leads or improvised connections.</p>']],['★ State workshop safety rules.','★ Explain uses of vernier caliper and micrometer.','★ Why is zero error important?','★ List good housekeeping practices.'],'Safety first → correct tool → secure work → measure → inspect.' );

add('Carpentry and Wood Working','Carpentry involves measuring, marking, cutting, shaping and joining wood to produce useful components.',[
['1. Wood and Grain','<p>Wood contains fibres whose direction affects strength, cutting and surface finish. Moisture content influences dimensional stability and service behaviour.</p>'],
['2. Tools','<p>Common tools include saws, chisels, planes, hammers, try squares, marking gauges and measuring tools. Each tool should be used for its intended operation.</p>'],
['3. Joints','<p>Common joints include butt, lap, mortise-and-tenon and dovetail joints. Joint selection depends on load, appearance, required strength and manufacturing method.</p>'],
['4. Safety','<p>Secure the workpiece, keep hands away from cutting paths, use sharp tools correctly and maintain a clean workbench. Eye protection is appropriate where chips or dust may be produced.</p>']],['★ List common carpentry tools.','★ Explain mortise-and-tenon joint.','★ State carpentry safety rules.'],'Measure → mark → cut → shape → join → finish.' );

add('Joining and Welding Processes','Joining processes connect materials either permanently or temporarily. Welding produces strong permanent joints by heat, pressure or both.',[
['1. Welding Principle','<p>In fusion welding, the joint region is heated until material melts and then solidifies. A filler metal may be supplied. In solid-state processes, joining occurs without complete melting of the base materials.</p>'],
['2. Arc Welding','<p>An electric arc produces heat between an electrode and workpiece. Shielding may protect the molten metal from atmospheric contamination. Correct current, electrode selection and travel speed influence weld quality.</p>'],
['3. Gas Welding','<p>Gas welding uses a flame produced by combustion of a fuel gas with oxygen. It is useful for suitable repair, thin-section and fabrication work.</p>'],
['4. Soldering and Brazing','<p>Soldering and brazing use filler metals whose melting temperatures are below those of the base materials. They differ mainly in filler melting-temperature range and joint requirements.</p>']],['★ Define welding.','★ Explain arc welding.','★ Compare gas welding, soldering and brazing.','★ State factors affecting weld quality.'],'Select process → prepare joint → control heat → protect joint → inspect.' );

add('Electrical and Electronics Workshop Practices','Workshop practice introduces safe wiring, component identification, soldering and basic electrical/electronic testing.',[
['1. Electrical Tools and Safety','<p>Common tools include tester, pliers, wire stripper, screwdrivers, soldering iron and multimeter. Before working on a circuit, isolate the supply where appropriate and verify the absence of dangerous voltage.</p>'],
['2. Wiring','<p>Wiring practice includes correct conductor identification, insulation, terminals, switches, protective devices and proper mechanical support. Connections must be tight and insulated.</p>'],
['3. Electronic Components','<p>Identify resistors, capacitors, inductors, diodes, LEDs, transistors and switches by their physical markings and electrical symbols. Component ratings must be respected.</p>'],
['4. Soldering and Multimeter','<p>A good soldered joint is clean, mechanically stable and adequately wetted. A multimeter can measure voltage, resistance and current when used with the correct connection and range. Never place an ammeter directly across a voltage source.</p>']],['★ List electrical workshop safety rules.','★ Identify common electronic components.','★ Explain a good solder joint.','★ State safe multimeter practices.'],'Safety → identify → connect → test → inspect.' );

add('Fire Safety and First Aid','Fire safety focuses on preventing ignition, recognising fire hazards and responding appropriately while first aid provides immediate assistance until trained medical help is available.',[
['1. Fire Triangle','<p>Fire requires <b>fuel, heat and oxygen</b>. Removing one of these elements can stop combustion. Different fire classes involve different fuels, so the extinguishing method must be suitable for the fire.</p>'],
['2. Prevention','<p>Keep flammable materials away from ignition sources, maintain electrical equipment, avoid overloaded circuits and store chemicals/fuels according to safety procedures. Emergency exits and extinguishers must remain accessible.</p>'],
['3. Emergency Response','<p>Raise the alarm, move away from danger and follow the building emergency procedure. Do not take unnecessary risks to fight a fire. Use an extinguisher only when trained, the fire is small and an escape route remains available.</p>'],
['4. First Aid Principles','<p>First aid begins with scene safety and getting appropriate help. For burns, cool the affected area with clean running water and avoid applying household substances to the wound. Serious injuries require professional medical attention.</p>']],['★ Explain the fire triangle.','★ List fire-prevention measures.','★ State safe emergency response steps.','★ Explain basic first-aid principles for burns.'],'Prevent → alert → evacuate → assist safely → obtain professional help.' );

add('Professional Ethics','Professional ethics are principles that guide responsible behaviour in technical and workplace environments.',[
['1. Values and Ethics','<p>Values are principles considered important by an individual or society. Ethics provides standards for deciding what conduct is responsible or acceptable in a professional context.</p>'],
['2. Professional Responsibility','<p>Engineers and technicians are expected to value safety, honesty, competence, confidentiality, respect, reliability and responsibility. Technical decisions can affect users, workers, the environment and society.</p>'],
['3. Ethical Decision Making','<p>Identify the facts, affected stakeholders, safety risks, professional duties and applicable rules. Consider alternatives and choose an action that can be justified on professional and ethical grounds.</p>']],['★ Define professional ethics.','★ Explain professional responsibility.','★ List important professional values.','★ Describe an ethical decision-making process.'],'Competence + honesty + safety + responsibility + respect.' );

add('Sports and Exercises','Physical activity supports general fitness, coordination and healthy daily functioning.',[
['1. Components of Fitness','<p>Important components include cardiovascular endurance, muscular strength, muscular endurance, flexibility, balance, coordination and suitable body composition.</p>'],
['2. Exercise Principles','<p>Exercise should be progressive and appropriate to the individual. Warm-up prepares the body for activity; cool-down allows gradual recovery. Hydration, rest and safe technique are important.</p>'],
['3. Sportsmanship','<p>Good sportsmanship includes fair play, respect for rules and opponents, teamwork and responsible behaviour. Training should prioritise safe technique rather than unnecessary competition.</p>']],['★ List components of physical fitness.','★ Explain warm-up and cool-down.','★ What is sportsmanship?'],'Regular activity + safe technique + recovery + consistency.' );

add('Yoga and Meditation','Yoga and meditation are practices involving physical postures, breathing, attention and relaxation.',[
['1. Yoga','<p>Yoga practices may include asanas, controlled breathing and relaxation. Correct posture and gradual practice are more important than forcing a position.</p>'],
['2. Meditation','<p>Meditation trains attention and awareness. A basic practice may involve sitting comfortably, observing breathing and returning attention gently when the mind wanders.</p>'],
['3. Wellness','<p>Healthy routines also include adequate sleep, balanced nutrition, regular physical activity and appropriate stress-management strategies. Yoga or meditation should complement, not replace, professional care when a health problem requires it.</p>']],['★ Define yoga and meditation.','★ Explain benefits of regular practice.','★ State basic principles of safe practice.'],'Posture + breathing + attention + consistency.' );

add('Fitness and Wellness','Wellness is a broad concept involving physical, mental, social and daily-life habits.',[
['1. Physical Wellness','<p>Regular activity, adequate sleep, hydration, balanced meals and safe habits support physical wellbeing.</p>'],
['2. Mental and Social Wellness','<p>Healthy routines include managing academic workload, maintaining supportive relationships, taking breaks and seeking appropriate help when problems become difficult to manage.</p>'],
['3. Personal Routine','<p>A practical routine balances study, physical activity, rest, recreation and responsibilities. Consistency is more useful than extreme schedules that cannot be maintained.</p>']],['★ Define wellness.','★ Explain components of wellness.','★ Suggest features of a balanced daily routine.'],'Wellness is a balance of sustainable habits, not a single activity.' );

add('Environment and Ecosystems','An ecosystem is a functional unit in which living organisms interact with one another and with physical environmental factors.',[
['1. Ecosystem Components','<p><b>Biotic components</b> include producers, consumers and decomposers. <b>Abiotic components</b> include light, temperature, water, soil, air and minerals.</p>'],
['2. Food Chain and Food Web','<p>Energy enters most ecosystems through producers and moves through trophic levels. A food web connects multiple food chains. Energy is lost at each transfer, so higher trophic levels generally contain less available energy.</p>'],
['3. Ecological Balance','<p>Population changes, habitat loss, pollution and excessive resource use can disturb ecosystems. Conservation seeks to maintain biodiversity and ecosystem functions.</p>']],['★ Define ecosystem.','★ Differentiate biotic and abiotic components.','★ Explain food chain and food web.','★ What causes ecological imbalance?'],'Abiotic environment + organisms + interactions + energy flow = ecosystem.' );

add('Natural Resources','Natural resources are materials and environmental services obtained from nature and used by society.',[
['1. Renewable and Non-Renewable','<p>Solar energy, wind and sustainably managed biological resources are renewable when their use does not exceed natural regeneration. Coal, petroleum and many mineral deposits are non-renewable on human time scales.</p>'],
['2. Water and Forest Resources','<p>Water conservation includes efficient use, rainwater harvesting, reuse and protection of water bodies. Forests provide biodiversity, soil protection, carbon storage and livelihood-related services.</p>'],
['3. Sustainable Use','<p>Resource management should consider present needs, regeneration rates, environmental impacts and the needs of future generations.</p>']],['★ Classify natural resources.','★ Compare renewable and non-renewable resources.','★ Explain water conservation.','★ Why are forests important?'],'Use resources efficiently and protect their regenerative capacity.' );

add('Pollution and Control','Pollution occurs when harmful substances or energy alter environmental conditions and cause adverse effects.',[
['1. Air Pollution','<p>Major air pollutants include particulate matter, nitrogen oxides, sulphur oxides, carbon monoxide and ground-level ozone precursors. Sources include transport, combustion, industry and dust.</p><p>Control methods include cleaner fuels, process modification, filtration, electrostatic precipitation and emission standards where applicable.</p>'],
['2. Water Pollution','<p>Water pollution may result from sewage, industrial effluents, agricultural runoff and waste. Indicators include dissolved oxygen, biochemical oxygen demand and chemical oxygen demand.</p>'],
['3. Soil and Noise Pollution','<p>Improper waste disposal, chemicals and excessive use of certain agricultural inputs can degrade soil. Noise pollution can affect communication, comfort and health. Source control, barriers and proper planning can reduce noise.</p>'],
['4. Waste Management','<p>The preferred hierarchy is generally prevention and reduction first, followed by reuse, recycling/recovery and appropriate treatment/disposal. Segregation at source improves waste management.</p>']],['★ Define pollution.','★ List major air pollutants.','★ Explain BOD and COD.','★ Describe the waste-management hierarchy.'],'Prevent pollution at source → reduce → reuse/recycle → treat → dispose responsibly.' );

add('Sustainable Development','Sustainable development aims to meet present needs while maintaining the environmental, social and economic conditions required for future generations.',[
['1. Three Dimensions','<p>Sustainability is commonly discussed through <b>environmental, social and economic</b> dimensions. A technically efficient project may still be unsustainable if it creates unacceptable environmental or social impacts.</p>'],
['2. Energy and Engineering','<p>Engineers contribute through energy efficiency, renewable-energy integration, resource-efficient design, durable products, pollution control and life-cycle thinking.</p>'],
['3. Climate Responsibility','<p>Climate change is linked to changes in atmospheric concentrations of greenhouse gases. Responsible engineering considers energy use, emissions, resilience and adaptation along with technical performance.</p>']],['★ Define sustainable development.','★ Explain the three dimensions of sustainability.','★ How can engineers support sustainable development?','★ Explain the importance of energy efficiency.'],'Sustainable design = technical performance + environmental responsibility + social and economic considerations.' );

add('Force Systems and Equilibrium','Engineering mechanics analyses forces and their effects on bodies. A body is in equilibrium when the resultant force and resultant moment satisfy the required equilibrium conditions.',[
['1. Force and Resultant','<p>A force has magnitude, direction and point of application. Forces can be resolved into components along convenient axes. The resultant is the vector sum of all applied forces.</p><div class="formula">For planar equilibrium: ΣFx=0, ΣFy=0, ΣM=0</div>'],
['2. Moment','<p>The moment of a force about a point measures its turning effect.</p><div class="formula">M = F × perpendicular distance</div><p>The sign depends on the selected clockwise/anticlockwise convention.</p>'],
['3. Free-Body Diagram','<p>A free-body diagram isolates the body and shows all external forces, reactions, weights and applied loads. It is the most important step before writing equilibrium equations.</p>']],['★ Define force and moment.','★ Resolve a force into components.','★ State equilibrium equations.','★ Draw a free-body diagram for a simple beam or block.'],'FBD → components → equilibrium equations → solve reactions/unknowns.' );

add('Friction','Friction is the resistance to relative motion or tendency of motion between contacting surfaces.',[
['1. Types','<p><b>Static friction</b> acts before sliding begins and adjusts up to a limiting value. <b>Kinetic friction</b> acts during sliding. Rolling resistance occurs in rolling contact and has different physical causes.</p>'],
['2. Laws and Coefficient','<p>For a simple dry-contact model, limiting friction is proportional to normal reaction:</p><div class="formula">Fₗ = μN</div><p>where μ is coefficient of friction. The friction force opposes the relative motion or its tendency.</p>'],
['3. Angle of Friction','<p>The angle of friction is related to the coefficient by:</p><div class="formula">tan φ = μ</div><p>Friction analysis is used in wedges, ladders, belts, brakes and machine elements.</p>']],['★ Define static and kinetic friction.','★ State the limiting-friction relation.','★ Explain angle of friction.','★ Solve a block-on-plane friction problem.'],'Normal reaction + friction direction + limiting condition are the key steps.' );

add('Centroid and Centre of Gravity','The centroid is the geometric centre of a plane area, while the centre of gravity is the point through which the resultant weight of a body acts.',[
['1. Centroid of Simple Areas','<p>For symmetrical uniform shapes, the centroid lies on the symmetry axis. Standard locations should be memorised for common rectangles, triangles, circles, semicircles and composite areas.</p>'],
['2. Composite Areas','<p>Divide an irregular area into simple component areas. Assign coordinates to each component centroid and use:</p><div class="formula">x̄ = Σ(Ax)/ΣA<br>ȳ = Σ(Ay)/ΣA</div><p>For holes, treat the removed area as negative area.</p>'],
['3. Centre of Gravity','<p>For a uniform body in a uniform gravitational field, the centre of gravity coincides with its centre of mass. For composite bodies, weighted averages of component locations can be used.</p>']],['★ Define centroid.','★ Derive the composite-area centroid formula.','★ Find centroid of a composite section.','★ Differentiate centroid and centre of gravity.'],'Split → assign area → locate component centroid → apply ΣAx/ΣA and ΣAy/ΣA.' );

add('Simple Lifting Machines','A lifting machine helps raise a load by applying a smaller effort, while mechanical advantage and efficiency describe its performance.',[
['1. Mechanical Advantage','<div class="formula">MA = Load / Effort</div><p>Mechanical advantage measures the multiplication of input force. It does not by itself indicate how much input distance is required.</p>'],
['2. Velocity Ratio','<div class="formula">VR = distance moved by effort / distance moved by load</div><p>For an ideal machine, output work equals input work and MA equals VR. Real machines have losses.</p>'],
['3. Efficiency','<div class="formula">η = MA/VR × 100%</div><p>Efficiency is less than 100% for practical machines because of friction and other losses.</p>']],['★ Define MA and VR.','★ Derive efficiency relation.','★ Solve a lifting-machine numerical.','★ Explain why practical efficiency is below 100%.'],'Load/Effort → MA; effort distance/load distance → VR; MA/VR → efficiency.' );

add('Kinematics and Dynamics','Kinematics describes motion without considering its causes; dynamics relates motion to forces and mass.',[
['1. Basic Motion','<p>Displacement is a vector change in position. Velocity is rate of change of displacement and acceleration is rate of change of velocity.</p><div class="formula">v = ds/dt<br>a = dv/dt = d²s/dt²</div>'],
['2. Uniform Acceleration','<div class="formula">v=u+at<br>s=ut+½at²<br>v²=u²+2as</div><p>These equations apply when acceleration is constant.</p>'],
['3. Newton’s Laws','<p>Newton’s first law describes inertia. The second relates net force to rate of change of momentum and, for constant mass, gives <b>F=ma</b>. The third law states that interacting bodies exert equal and opposite forces on each other.</p>'],
['4. Work, Power and Energy','<div class="formula">Work = Force × displacement in force direction<br>Power = Work/time</div><p>Kinetic energy is associated with motion; potential energy is associated with position or configuration. Conservation of energy links mechanical processes.</p>']],['★ Differentiate displacement, velocity and acceleration.','★ Derive one equation of uniformly accelerated motion.','★ State Newton’s three laws.','★ Define work, power and energy.'],'Position → velocity → acceleration; force causes acceleration according to dynamics.' );

add('Indian Constitution','The Indian Constitution provides the basic framework of government, rights, duties and institutional relationships in India.',[
['1. Constitution and Preamble','<p>The Constitution is the supreme legal framework for the functioning of the Republic. The Preamble states the broad constitutional objectives and describes India as a sovereign, socialist, secular, democratic republic committed to justice, liberty, equality and fraternity.</p>'],
['2. Fundamental Rights','<p>Fundamental Rights are constitutional guarantees subject to the conditions and limitations specified by the Constitution. They include equality-related rights, freedoms, protection against exploitation, freedom of religion, cultural and educational rights and constitutional remedies.</p>'],
['3. Directive Principles','<p>Directive Principles of State Policy guide the State in policy-making toward social and economic welfare. They are important constitutional principles but differ in legal enforceability from Fundamental Rights.</p>'],
['4. Constitutional Amendments','<p>The Constitution contains a formal amendment procedure. Different provisions may require different procedures, including special majorities and, for certain federal matters, ratification by a specified number of State legislatures.</p>']],['★ Explain the purpose of the Preamble.','★ List the broad categories of Fundamental Rights.','★ Differentiate Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles.','★ What is a constitutional amendment?'],'Preamble → rights → directive principles → institutions → amendment framework.' );

add('Open Educational Resources','Open Educational Resources are teaching, learning and research materials made available under public-domain status or an open licence that permits specified reuse.',[
['1. What Makes an OER','<p>An OER is not simply a free-to-view webpage. Its legal status must permit the uses claimed by the resource. Licence conditions determine whether copying, adaptation, redistribution or commercial use is allowed.</p>'],
['2. Creative Commons','<p>Creative Commons licences communicate permissions in a standard way. <b>CC BY</b> permits broad reuse with attribution. <b>CC BY-SA</b> adds a share-alike condition. Other licences add restrictions such as non-commercial use or no derivatives.</p>'],
['3. Copyright and Attribution','<p>Copyright normally gives the creator exclusive rights subject to applicable law and exceptions. When using an openly licensed work, preserve the required attribution and comply with the exact licence conditions. “Available online” does not automatically mean “free to copy”.</p>'],
['4. OER Repositories and Quality','<p>Repositories help users discover, organise and share educational materials. Quality checks can include authorship, peer review, metadata, keywords, licence clarity, source-file availability, user evaluation and accessibility.</p>']],['★ Define OER.','★ Explain CC BY and CC BY-SA.','★ Why is attribution important?','★ Does an online resource automatically become OER? Explain.'],'Open access ≠ automatically open licence. Always check the licence and attribution requirements.' );
})();